Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream is(markdown);

    while (std::getline(is, line)) {
        html += line;
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello World\n\nThis is a paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and appends each line to an HTML string. The `std::getline` function is used to read each line from the stream. The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function with a sample markdown text.

Note that this function does not support all markdown features, such as images or links. For a more robust implementation, you may want to use a markdown library like Pandoc or MarkDown.

